                <h2>Step 1: Diagnose—Is Your Copy Already Homogenized?</h2>
<p>AI-generated copy often has a distinct "AI flavor"—logically sound but emotionally flat. When competitors in the same niche use similar prompts, the output becomes nearly identical.</p>
<p><strong>Quick Self-Check (2+ yes = homogenization risk):</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Core value propositions overlap heavily with competitors (e.g., "Premium Quality," "Limited Time Offer")</li>
<li>CTR drops within 48 hours of launch</li>
<li>User comments mention "looks just like [competitor's] ad"</li>
<li>CTR difference between AI-generated and manually revised versions is less than 5%</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Step 2: Inject Brand-Specific Language</h2>
<p>AI excels at generic phrasing but lacks your brand voice. <strong>Build a brand vocabulary list</strong> and embed it in every prompt.</p>
<p><strong>How to do it:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>Extract 2-3 differentiating keywords from core selling points</li>
<li>Gather real user language from comments and social media</li>
<li>Add this instruction at the start of your prompt: "Use the following brand expressions: [specific terms]"</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> A utility app replaced "Easy to Use" with "works for ages 3 to 80," boosting CTR by ~18%.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>Step 3: Add Emotional Triggers</h2>
<p>Purely informational copy has limited persuasive power. Emotional triggers elevate copy from "product description" to "user story."</p>
<p><strong>Four High-Impact Triggers:</strong></p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Type</th>
<th>Effect</th>
<th>Application</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Urgency</td>
<td>Drives immediate action</td>
<td>"Only XX spots left this week" (avoid overuse)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Social Proof</td>
<td>Reduces decision risk</td>
<td>"XX users are already using this"</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Personalization</td>
<td>Enhances relevance</td>
<td>"Users like you are switching to..."</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Contrast</td>
<td>Grabs attention</td>
<td>"Everyone else does XX, only you..."</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Tip:</strong> Explicitly specify the emotion type in your prompt, e.g., "Generate a TikTok ad with mild urgency targeting 25-35-year-old working women."</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 4: Break Fixed Structures</h2>
<p>AI defaults to the "pain point → product → CTA" three-paragraph format. <strong>Actively disrupting this structure</strong> significantly reduces similarity with competitors.</p>
<p><strong>Three High-Converting Structure Variants:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Inverted Pyramid</strong>: Lead with the conclusion (e.g., "Save 50%"), then provide evidence, end with CTA</li>
<li><strong>Dialogue Format</strong>: Simulate Q&amp;A, ideal for Meta Messenger and DM ads</li>
<li><strong>Story Format</strong>: Open with a specific scenario (e.g., "Last week, a customer named Zhang..."), then naturally introduce the product</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Step 5: Incorporate Real User Feedback</h2>
<p>AI fabricates generic testimonials well, but users trust real data. <strong>Embedding authentic feedback snippets</strong> dramatically improves credibility.</p>
<p><strong>Best Practices:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>Extract real language from post-purchase reviews and social posts</li>
<li>Keep colloquial expressions—don't over-polish</li>
<li>Cite the data source (e.g., "Based on feedback from XX users")</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> "Tried it for 3 months before recommending it" converted ~23% better than "High quality, trustworthy" (A/B test data from a cross-border beauty brand).</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>Step 6: Validate Differentiation with A/B Testing</h2>
<p>Whether your copy is truly differentiated <strong>ultimately depends on data</strong>.</p>
<p><strong>Minimum Viable Test Setup:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Upload 2-3 copy versions with identical audience and budget</li>
<li>Key metrics: CTR, 2-second play rate (TikTok), CVR</li>
<li>Sign of effective differentiation: AI version CTR exceeds manual version by 10%+</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Execution Checklist</h2>
<p>Before launching, verify:</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Contains at least 2 brand-specific expressions</li>
<li>[ ] Embeds 1+ emotional triggers</li>
<li>[ ] Uses a non-standard structure</li>
<li>[ ] Includes real data or user quotes</li>
<li>[ ] Validated through A/B testing</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<p><strong>Bottom Line:</strong> AI is a productivity tool, not a creative endpoint. Following this 5-step SOP helps maintain output volume while producing differentiated creatives. The key is building a brand vocabulary, actively breaking AI's default structures, incorporating real user data, and always validating results through testing.</p>
